  {
   "id": "L1.1",
   "unit": 1,
   "title": "One word, several pieces",
   "unlocks": [
    "M:ve"
   ],
   "body": "Hebrew attaches small words to the front of the next word: and, the, in, to, like, from.\n{0} is and + the + earth.\n\nRead from the right edge of the Hebrew: peel off prefixes until the core word is left. In\nreadings the app colors the prefixes so you can see the split.\n\nMost words carry at most two prefixes. The core is what you look up.",
   "refs": [
    {
     "ids": [
      "01LN3"
     ],
     "hl": null
    }
   ],
   "words": 67
  },
  {
   "id": "L1.2",
   "unit": 1,
   "title": "And: ve-, u-, va-",
   "unlocks": [
    "M:ve",
    "M:u",
    "M:va",
    "M:vayy"
   ],
   "body": "The vav prefix means \"and\" (sometimes \"but\", \"so\", \"then\").\n\nUsually ve-: {0}.\nBefore b, m, p or a shva it becomes u-: {1}, {2}.\nSometimes va-: {3}.\n\nOn story verbs it is va- with the next letter doubled: {4}. For now learn\nthose verbs as whole words; Unit 3 takes them apart.",
